35,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"The closeout files are sobering. Catalogue-style awards still serve users a decade later, while mechanism-focused ones often ended unresolved.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The staffing data is stark. Phenotyping technicians are gone, but the glasshouse crew remains intact. This shifts feasibility toward field-based work.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Victoria,,The spectroscopy suite sat idle for eleven weeks. That is a critical bottleneck for any project relying on that specific instrumentation.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"The computing agreement covers simulation free, but animal-unit rates spiked. Meanwhile, glasshouse bench fees remain subsidized. This asymmetry heavily favors computational or plant-based work over animal models.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Midori,,"The field sites lost whole seasons to weather, but indoor rooms ran uninterrupted. That confirms my worry about external interruptions.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"Ryuji, your staffing point aligns with my findings. Live breeding colonies overrun schedules twice as often as computational work. We must weigh that risk carefully.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"Shizue, that confirms my concern. With senior analysts abroad and technicians gone, we cannot rely on complex modelling. We need a project the remaining glasshouse crew can execute independently.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Victoria,,The glasshouse blocks are booked solid for two more cycles. That capacity is gone. We cannot fund a project requiring that space.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Victoria, that contradicts the ledger data. The ministry extended the bench-fee subsidy specifically for glasshouse space. Are you certain the bookings are fully saturated?","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Victoria, if the glasshouses are truly full, that rules out the plant proposal. But the indoor rooms stayed open. Does that mean the amphibian work is safe from weather delays?","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"Midori, the indoor rooms did stay open, but the breeding colonies still overrun schedules. I worry the amphibian work carries that same biological risk.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"Midori, the amphibian work is risky. The technicians are gone, and the analysts are abroad. We lack the specific hands to manage those colonies reliably.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Benjamin, the booking data is explicit: solid through two cycles. The subsidy covers cost, not capacity. We cannot fund a project requiring that space.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Victoria, you’re conflating cost with capacity. The subsidy confirms the agency still absorbs the expense, but I need to know if the physical space is actually available.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, you’re right. The subsidy covers cost, not space. If the glasshouses are full, the plant proposal is blocked. We must look elsewhere.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Shizue,,"I appreciate the clarity, Victoria. The glasshouse constraint is real. Given the staffing gaps and biological risks, I am leaning toward the computational or catalogue-based options.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"I concur. With the glasshouse saturated and technical staff depleted, the plant proposal is unviable. We must prioritize projects executable by the remaining computational and indoor teams.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Victoria,,I agree. The spectroscopy suite’s eleven-week idle period is a severe risk for the protein folding proposal. We cannot rely on that equipment.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I agree. The subsidy confirms central cost absorption, but Victoria’s capacity data is decisive. The plant proposal is blocked. We must prioritize projects executable by the remaining computational and indoor teams.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I agree. The field site losses confirm that outdoor work is too risky. With glasshouses full and spectroscopy unreliable, the microbiome atlas seems safest.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
