66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,I concur. The atlas requires no glasshouse space or spectroscopy. It fits the remaining staff and avoids the biological risks we identified. I support D.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Victoria,,The spectroscopy risk eliminates C. The glasshouse saturation eliminates A. The staffing gaps eliminate B. D is the only viable option. I support D.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"The cost structure favors D. No animal rates, no glasshouse fees, and free computing for any analysis. It is the only option that doesn't bleed money on central costs. I support D.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Midori,,I agree. The atlas sidesteps the interruptions I feared. It is the most stable path forward. I support D.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Shizue,,I am content. The evidence points clearly to D. It aligns with our shared constraints and past successes. I will vote for the Microbiome Atlas.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
